  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 300 AM CDT Wed Oct 7 2026\n\nCurrently, another trough is beginning to dig into the northeastern \nU.S. while weak ridging extends across the western portions of the \ncountry. Ahead of the mid/upper level trough, a weak surface trough \nis developing across the Upper Midwest into the Plains. The \nassociated frontal boundary will march southward through the day \ntoday, reaching our central Kansas counties this afternoon. This \nwill lead to temperatures a couple of degrees cooler for the \nnorthern portions of the forecast area today and tomorrow. Highs in \nthe lower 80s are anticipated for central Kansas while temperatures \nwill reach into the middle to upper 80s for south-central and \nsoutheastern Kansas. Behind this weak boundary, winds will also \nshift out of the northeast over the course of the afternoon/evening, \nremaining light. \n\nWhile anomalous heights will lead to afternoon temperatures reaching \nabove normal through the end of the forecast period, the surface \nboundary associated with the northeastern US trough will linger over \ncentral Kansas through Thursday. Highs across south-central and \nsoutheast Kansas will once again reach into the middle to upper 80s \nfor Thursday while highs over central Kansas will remain in the \nlower 80s. The boundary will lift north as a warm front by Friday, \nleading to middle to upper 80s areawide for Fri-Sun. By Saturday, a \ntrough will begin digging into the Pac NW and a corresponding \nsurface low will develop over the Northern Rockies. The resulting \ntightening pressure gradient over the Plains will lead to breezy \nsouthwesterly winds for the weekend. Sustained winds between 15-25 \nmph with gusts up to 35 mph are possible both Saturday and Sunday.\n\nThe next chance for a pattern change will then come next week as a \ncutoff low develops over southern California on Monday and lifts \ntoward the Plains by mid week. While confidence in exact timing and \nimpacts remains low, continuity between model guidance suggests \nincreased rain chances and cooler temperatures for the tail end of \nthe forecast time frame and beyond.",
